DO  - 10.3389/fspor.2024.1429789
AN  - PMID:39205815
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39205815/
N2  - A Bayesian meta-analysis of 19 measurements from 9 randomised studies found a small muscle-growth benefit from resting more than 60 s rather than 60 s or less. This was possibly because longer rests let people lift more total volume. There was no appreciable extra benefit beyond about 90 s, and the whole-body estimate was -0.08 (95% credible interval -0.45 to 0.29).
N1  - Systematic review with Bayesian meta-analysis of randomised studies comparing inter-set rest durations in healthy adults, with other training variables controlled; 19 measurements (thigh, arm, whole body) from 9 studies. Singer, Wolf, Schoenfeld et al., Front Sports Act Living 2024 (published 14 Aug 2024), doi:10.3389/fspor.2024.1429789.. Verified 2026-10-14. Used for: training.inter_set_rest: supports
